1
00:00:00,130 --> 00:00:01,540
How fast is rain?

2
00:00:01,660 --> 00:00:03,685
It starts far above,
in clouds.

3
00:00:04,153 --> 00:00:05,818
A stone dropped
from a roof

4
00:00:05,818 --> 00:00:08,093
keeps speeding up
until it lands.

5
00:00:08,263 --> 00:00:09,903
A raindrop doesn't.

6
00:00:10,118 --> 00:00:12,648
Watch the ghosts:
the gaps grow,

7
00:00:12,648 --> 00:00:14,078
then stop growing.

8
00:00:14,458 --> 00:00:17,323
It reaches its top
speed almost at once.

9
00:00:17,987 --> 00:00:19,852
The air pushes back.

10
00:00:19,852 --> 00:00:22,397
You feel it with a hand
out of a car window.

11
00:00:22,812 --> 00:00:26,437
The faster the drop falls,
the harder the air pushes.

12
00:00:27,153 --> 00:00:29,788
It's a tug of war between
the weight pulling down

13
00:00:29,788 --> 00:00:31,783
and the air pushing up.

14
00:00:31,783 --> 00:00:33,043
At some speed,

15
00:00:33,043 --> 00:00:36,093
the push of the air
matches the drop's weight.

16
00:00:36,098 --> 00:00:39,133
After that,
the speed stays the same.

17
00:00:39,887 --> 00:00:42,847
Big drops fall faster
than small ones.

18
00:00:43,037 --> 00:00:44,957
Raindrops are never
teardrops.

19
00:00:45,622 --> 00:00:47,222
Small ones are round,

20
00:00:47,362 --> 00:00:49,507
and the biggest
flatten like a bun,

21
00:00:49,507 --> 00:00:51,012
and then break apart.

22
00:00:51,687 --> 00:00:53,922
Measure it:
in this model,

23
00:00:53,952 --> 00:00:55,872
a 1-millimeter
drop tops out

24
00:00:55,872 --> 00:00:58,107
near 5 meters a second.

25
00:00:58,262 --> 00:01:00,787
A 4-millimeter drop,
near nine.

26
00:01:01,353 --> 00:01:04,008
The air gives every drop
a speed limit.

27
00:01:04,173 --> 00:01:07,018
So rain from way up
high lands gently.

28
00:01:07,787 --> 00:01:09,212
That's season two.

29
00:01:09,357 --> 00:01:11,654
Thanks for watching,
and see you next time.
